 "paragraph": "Re-linearised steps and intermediate damping (post hoc). A post-hoc extension, with its analysis plan fixed by hash before any run, tested this interpretation on the 72 B1 units. Their fit, calibration and held-out sets were regenerated from the frozen B1 seeds with the B1 code tree of record, and at κ ≤ 0.01 the re-computed HQ-Fisher and TAN gains matched the stored values in every unit (largest relative difference 3 × 10⁻⁹). Five units (three two-layer, two three-layer) exceeded the planned tolerance of 10⁻⁶ only for HQ-Fisher at κ = 0.05, where the calibrated step scale reached 6 × 10⁸; the circuit angles are periodic, so such steps are numerically ill-posed, and this stress level enters no test of the extension. As the plan requires, the five units were excluded from every contrast, leaving 33 two-layer and 34 three-layer units. The circuit's damped natural step was then iterated in re-linearised sub-steps (HQ-Fisher-m, m = 4 or 16): at each sub-step the importance-weighted gradient and the Fisher were recomputed on the fit set at the current policy, the damped natural step was taken through the circuit with a KL of κ/m² on the calibration set, and sub-steps continued until the total mean forward KL from the initial policy reached κ (for m = 16 at κ = 0.01, a median of 32 sub-steps, range 19–97). At κ = 0.01, HQ-Fisher-16 differed from the single step by +1.47 [+0.53, +2.41] × 10⁻⁵ (two-sided p = 0.0033) and from TAN by −4.72 [−7.22, −2.23] × 10⁻⁵ (p = 5.3 × 10⁻⁴); both contrasts were rejected after Holm adjustment over the two in the two-layer stratum. In the three-layer stratum they were +1.54 [+0.41, +2.66] (p = 0.009) and −4.36 [−6.78, −1.94] (p = 8.6 × 10⁻⁴), and with the excluded units reinstated (not in the plan) the two-layer contrasts were +1.41 [+0.53, +2.30] and −4.29 [−6.62, −1.95]. Re-linearising the circuit step therefore recovered part of the shortfall, and part remained. Descriptively (a ratio not in the plan), HQ-Fisher-16 closed 24% (95% bootstrap interval 11–41%) of the single-step gap to TAN in the two-layer stratum (1.47 of 6.19 × 10⁻⁵ in the same units) and 26% (10–47%) in the three-layer stratum; at κ = 0.001 the shares were 47% and 59%. With m = 4 (median 6 sub-steps) the difference to TAN at κ = 0.01 was −6.12 [−8.69, −3.55] × 10⁻⁵, against −6.19 for the single step, so the recovered share was still rising at m = 16. The net coefficient displacement of HQ-Fisher-16 was nearly orthogonal to the initial direction (median cosine −0.04), whereas TAN-16, the same iteration inside the initial tangent span applied linearly, stayed aligned with it (0.99), reached κ in a median of 16 sub-steps and differed from TAN by −0.40 [−0.67, −0.13] × 10⁻⁵. The iterated circuit update therefore does not retrace the initial direction, whereas TAN continues along it in coefficient space, where the circuit need not be able to follow; this experiment does not separate the size of the realised steps from the curvature of the set of policies the circuit can reach as causes of the remaining gap. Over a damping ladder, HQ-Fisher − TAN at κ = 0.01 in the 33 retained two-layer units was δ = 10⁻⁶: −6.19 [−8.87, −3.51]; δ = 10⁻⁴: −6.33 [−8.85, −3.80]; δ = 10⁻²: −5.96 [−8.34, −3.59]; δ = 1: −5.49 [−8.13, −2.86] (× 10⁻⁵; δ = 10⁻⁶ is the B1 setting); at this KL, the shortfall of the circuit-realised step is therefore not specific to near-zero damping. Outside this primary cell the difference was unresolved for δ = 1 at κ = 0.001 in the three-layer stratum (−0.28 [−0.68, +0.12]) and for the plain step at κ = 0.001 in the three-layer stratum (+0.08 [−0.36, +0.53]). Appendix A lists every arm.",
